# Known signing keys, one machine per line — tracked, public keys only.
#
# This is what lets `git log --show-signature` say "Good signature" for a commit
# made on one of your *other* machines. Git is pointed at the generated
# ~/.ssh/allowed_signers, which steps/shared/42-git-signing.sh builds as this
# file plus the local machine's own key. So a machine always trusts itself with
# no commit required, and trusts its siblings once their key is added here.
#
# Format is ssh-keygen's, `principal keytype key`. To add a machine, run
# `./install.sh git-signing` there, copy the line it prints, add it below and
# commit. Removing a line revokes local trust; it does not touch GitHub — see
# "Commit signing" in the README for the full rotate/revoke path.
#
# Deliberately not auto-appended by the installer: writing to a tracked file is
# exactly the thing this repo is trying to stop doing.
